Now I have enough context. Let me check one detail about the existing `_NUMERIC_LITERAL_RE` regex correctness, especially around `0.5` matching when `is_residual = false` is also in the TOML (since the parser only sees the condition string from the inside of the section).

Actually — the regex is applied only to `outcome.get("condition", "")`, not the whole TOML, so the `is_residual = false` is not a concern. But I should check whether the regex would catch `1=1` (TC for residual) correctly per the spec claim.

Looking at the regex `(?<![a-zA-Z_])\b-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:[eE][+-]?\d+)?\b` against `"1=1"`: this would match `1` twice. The spec says "Residual catch-all outcomes typically use `TRUE` or `1=1` — no numeric literals, no warning." That's INCORRECT — the regex WILL match `1` in `1=1`. This is a bug between spec rationale and test coverage. There's no test for `1=1` residual conditions. Let me also check TC-3 behavior: `condition = "reproduced = TRUE"` — `TRUE` not numeric, fine. And TC-4b: `"output_path IS NOT NULL"` — fine.

Let me also assess one more thing — `bth new-experiment` scaffold uses `condition = "true"` (lowercase) for residual. That won't match. But existing real sidecars in projects may use `condition = "1=1"` for residuals.

Let me finalize the critique now.

